WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.835
After breakfast the next morning, it was time to make another trip to the wreck with Fritz.

2
00:00:19.836 --> 00:00:24.640
I called Ernest and Jack because I wanted to give them some instructions before we left.

3
00:00:24.641 --> 00:00:26.724
But they had disappeared.

4
00:00:26.725 --> 00:00:32.126
"We're in need of potatoes," said my wife. "I guess they went to dig some up."

5
00:00:32.127 --> 00:00:37.948
"When they come back," I said, "you must scold them for leaving without telling us where they were going.

6
00:00:37.949 --> 00:00:42.644
They seem to have taken Turk along, so I suppose no harm will come to them.

7
00:00:42.645 --> 00:00:45.740
But they still shouldn't have gone without our permission."

8
00:00:45.741 --> 00:00:50.144
I was reluctant to leave my dear wife alone with little Franz.

9
00:00:50.145 --> 00:00:53.346
And she was never happy when we visited the wreck.

10
00:00:53.347 --> 00:00:55.844
But I promised her we would be careful.

11
00:00:55.845 --> 00:00:59.999
"We'll be back tomorrow," I called over my shoulder as we set out.

12
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.184
We had just crossed the bridge when suddenly Jack and Ernest burst out of their hiding place.

13
00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:07.792
"Surprise!" cried Ernest.

14
00:01:07.793 --> 00:01:10.282
"We're coming with you!" announced Jack.

15
00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:15.003
The two boys danced with delight at having fooled their older brother and me.

16
00:01:15.004 --> 00:01:20.015
"No, you must go back and take care of Mother and Franz," I said firmly.

17
00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:23.896
"Oh, Father, please may we come?" begged Ernest.

18
00:01:23.897 --> 00:01:26.698
"It'll be an adventure," said Jack.

19
00:01:26.699 --> 00:01:29.125
I couldn't let them come with us.

20
00:01:29.126 --> 00:01:32.921
The wreck might be dangerous, and we needed to get some work done.

21
00:01:32.922 --> 00:01:37.033
But I also couldn't find it in my heart to scold the two merry rascals.

22
00:01:37.034 --> 00:01:42.291
"Hurry home so Mother won't worry about you," I said.

23
00:01:42.292 --> 00:01:46.545
Fritz and I waited a few minutes to be sure the boys were really heading home.

24
00:01:46.546 --> 00:01:53.394
Then we finished our hike to Safety Bay, where we found our tub boat.

25
00:01:53.395 --> 00:01:59.453
The current carried us briskly out of the bay, and we were soon moored alongside the wreck.

26
00:01:59.454 --> 00:02:03.574
We scrambled on deck and began to make our plans.

27
00:02:03.575 --> 00:02:09.134
"We need to build a raft to carry things that are too large for the tub boat," I said.

28
00:02:09.135 --> 00:02:12.612
"Let's use these empty water casks," said Fritz.

29
00:02:12.613 --> 00:02:16.028
"There must be a dozen of them."

30
00:02:16.029 --> 00:02:21.882
We tightly closed the casks, pushed them overboard, and arranged them in rows of three.

31
00:02:21.883 --> 00:02:27.284
After securing the casks together with pieces of wood, we built a deck of planks.

32
00:02:27.285 --> 00:02:30.635
Building the raft took us all day.

33
00:02:30.636 --> 00:02:34.770
We made an excellent supper from the ship's provisions and then went to bed.

34
00:02:34.771 --> 00:02:41.950
That night we slept on thick, soft mattresses—a luxury after our hammocks.

35
00:02:41.951 --> 00:02:45.869
The next morning Fritz and I began loading the raft and tub boat.

36
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:52.270
We first carried off the entire contents of our own cabins, including those wonderful mattresses.

37
00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:57.812
From the captain's cabin, we removed the furniture as well as the doors and window frames.

38
00:02:57.813 --> 00:03:02.807
We then took the chests belonging to the ship's officers, carpenter, and gunsmith.

39
00:03:02.808 --> 00:03:10.745
"Look at this!" cried Fritz as he opened another chest filled with gold and silver watches, buckles, chains, and rings.

40
00:03:10.746 --> 00:03:12.864
"It's like a jeweler's shop."

41
00:03:12.865 --> 00:03:17.248
We also found a box of money, but that was of no use to us now.

42
00:03:17.249 --> 00:03:23.181
Of more interest was a group of young fruit trees: apple, pear, peach, cherry.

43
00:03:23.182 --> 00:03:29.549
There were plumber's tools, paint, cart wheels, sacks of peas, oats, and wheat.

44
00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:35.509
We even spotted all the parts of a sawmill, carefully numbered so that they could be put back together.

45
00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:40.564
We were so bewildered by the wealth around us that we couldn't decide what to take with us.

46
00:03:40.565 --> 00:03:43.003
"We can't take everything," I said.

47
00:03:43.004 --> 00:03:44.871
"Yes, Father," Fritz agreed.

48
00:03:44.872 --> 00:03:49.178
"But the first storm will destroy the wreck, and we'll lose anything we leave behind."

49
00:03:49.179 --> 00:03:50.759
Fritz was right.

50
00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:57.569
We made our choices, and our haul included fishing lines, reels, rope, and a mariner's compass.

51
00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:02.457
Remembering our encounter with the shark, Fritz insisted that we take a harpoon.

52
00:04:02.458 --> 00:04:07.767
Early in the afternoon both our vessels were heavily loaded, and we were ready to set sail.

53
00:04:07.768 --> 00:04:11.865
With the raft in tow, I was anxious that we might tip over.

54
00:04:11.866 --> 00:04:15.267
But the sea was calm and the wind was favorable.

55
00:04:15.268 --> 00:04:18.569
We spread our sail and soon neared shore.

56
00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:25.335
Fritz fired his gun to signal our arrival and not long after, the whole family arrived on the beach.

57
00:04:25.336 --> 00:04:30.499
We unloaded the raft and tub boat and began to carry some things back to Falconhurst.

58
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.399
When we arrived, Ernest was eager to show me some plants that he'd dug up.

59
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.031
"I don't know what they are," he said.

60
00:04:38.032 --> 00:04:43.282
"I didn't dare to taste them even though the pig was devouring them as fast as she could."

61
00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:45.734
"That was a wise decision," I said.

62
00:04:45.735 --> 00:04:49.837
Ernest brought me some of the plants, and I examined them thoroughly.

63
00:04:49.838 --> 00:04:56.876
"Why, Ernest, I believe you've made a brilliant discovery!"
